it has been nearly a month now since the  u.s food and drug administration issued  an alert which some nevada health  workers had already warned them about  covet antigen tests are prone to giving  false positive  denise valdez now joining us with  today's health watch denise  well hi there brian so how reliable are  these rapid tests that thousands of  people are getting  first let's start with the two standard  diagnostic tests and they are actually  quite different  first the molecular or the pcr now it's  pretty accurate  it's a lab-based test that can take days  to process and get the results  the test detects the viruses genetic  material  then the other is the antigen it's a  quick right on the spot test that's less  sensitive than the pcr  but it's pretty good at identifying  people who are at or near  peak infection now unlike those lab  based molecular pcr tests  they detect snippets of the viruses  material and  are less sensitive and they can detect  samples with a high viral load  now the tests were validated for people  with copin 19 symptoms  pretty much in the first week of  symptoms now in that scenario  they're pretty darn accurate but it's  trickier when it comes to asymptomatic  screenings  in fact about 40 percent of infections  are spread  by asymptomatic people with a high viral  load  so antigen tests even though they might  be a little imperfect  they shouldn't be dismissed dr kathleen  winston who is the dean for college of  nursing at university of phoenix  maintains widespread antigen testing is  still the key to curb the spread  you have to have a certain level of  virus in your bloodstream  in order for a diagnostic test to be  accurate so  again the the test that we're looking  for current infection  that's what the that's the measure of  the accuracy of that test  so if i have a high viral load i have  the the  nasopharyngeal swab test for the virus  then that will give me a  more closely correct and accurate  positive diagnosis  now depending on the quality of the  antigen test and also the test takers  false negatives could be as high as 20  percent  now the concern is false positives could  be caused by the presence of other  viruses  maybe they weren't collected properly or  even sometimes the swabs  may not have been stored at the proper  temperature still though  antigen testing technology continues to  improve and you should get tested if you  feel sick
 

